# Direct dependencies for this chapter's reader-facing teaching examples ONLY.
# Deliberately much smaller than slr_pipeline's own dependency surface (no
# langgraph, no docling, no full slr_pipeline import) -- these examples are
# self-contained, simplified teaching code, not thin wrappers around the
# production pipeline.
#
# SUPPORTED PLATFORM: Windows x86-64 only (matching this project's own
# actual, stated environment throughout CLAUDE.md -- PowerShell/Git-Bash on
# Windows are the only two shells this project documents). requirements.txt
# is a platform-specific lock for that platform, not a universal one --
# `uv pip compile --universal` was considered (round-3 review finding 3) and
# rejected: with bertopic/sentence-transformers/torch's own wide transitive
# graph, a genuinely universal resolution would weaken the version pins this
# lock is supposed to guarantee, for a project that has never targeted any
# platform other than Windows x86-64. If this teaching material is ever
# ported to another platform, generate and ship a second, clearly-named
# lock file for it rather than trying to make one file cover both.
#
# Tested against Python 3.12.12 (the lamai conda env already used throughout
# this project).
#
# This is the SOURCE list of direct dependencies -- resolve it into the real,
# hashed, transitive dependency lock (requirements.txt in this same
# directory) with the EXACT command below (verified: this is the actual
# command that produced the current requirements.txt, re-run from this
# directory so relative input/output paths resolve correctly):
#   uv pip compile requirements.in --generate-hashes --python-version 3.12 --native-tls -o requirements.txt
# (--native-tls works around this machine's Avast-intercepted TLS chain, per
# this project's own documented environment quirk; drop it on a machine
# without that interception -- the resulting package/version/hash set is
# unaffected either way, since it only changes how uv itself makes its own
# HTTPS requests during resolution, not what gets resolved.) Do not
# hand-edit requirements.txt.
#
# Mocking uses the standard-library unittest.mock -- no pytest-mock
# dependency (not installed in lamai, and unnecessary for this scope).
# pytest-socket (round-3 review finding 2) provides comprehensive network
# denial for the offline test contract (tests/chapter_examples/conftest.py)
# -- a real runtime dependency of running the documented default test
# command successfully, not just a repo-authoring-time tool.

openai==2.7.2
pydantic==2.12.4
bertopic==0.17.4
sentence-transformers==5.2.0
pytest==9.1.1
pytest-socket==0.8.1

